---
description: Tiered, replayable failure dumps for perception and planning
alwaysApply: true
---

# Debug evidence and dump granularity

The purpose of a dump is to locate the first divergent stage and reproduce the
failed case without another blind experiment. Do not dump every internal value
from every module. The default unit is:

`case identity x pipeline stage boundary x bounded trigger window`.

Stage boundaries are the inputs/outputs and join points between components.
Module-internal dumps are an escalation after boundary evidence identifies a
suspect module, not the default capture level.

## Required capture tiers

- **T0 -- manifest and result, every run:** commit and dirty count, case ID,
  config and seed/generated parameters, environment/profile, commands,
  start/end time, metric result, artifact inventory, and teardown status.
- **T1 -- lightweight boundary trace, every scored case:** timestamp, frame and
  request IDs, stage name, input/output identity, dimensions/counts, state and
  reason code, latency/rate, drop/join/buffer counters, and resource summaries.
  This trace must be cheap enough to remain enabled during scoring.
- **T2 -- failure-triggered replay bundle:** keep a bounded ring buffer; on a
  threshold failure, timeout, error reason, crash, or invariant violation,
  freeze the failing stage plus the minimum upstream input and downstream
  observation required to reproduce and localize it. Save a pre/post window
  sized by the plan from measured pipeline latency; do not choose an arbitrary
  whole-run dump.
- **T3 -- focused internal dump:** only when T2 localizes but cannot explain the
  fault. On the next instrumented attempt, enable internals for the named
  suspect module and case, such as RANSAC candidates, mask components, IK
  candidates, collision contacts, or planner search diagnostics. Record that
  this run has debug overhead and do not compare its timing to a clean score.

If the first failing stage is not yet known, T2 covers each stage boundary,
not every internal variable. Once known, narrow subsequent capture to that
stage, one upstream boundary, and the necessary downstream proof. A plan may
reduce sampling rate, window length, or use compression/chunking to meet an
explicit I/O and disk budget, but it may not omit replay-critical data.

## Dump matrix required in every eval plan

Before dispatch, the plan must state for every relevant boundary:

| Boundary | T1 trace | Failure trigger | T2/T3 payload | Pre/post window | Format and size budget | Replay/inspection command |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|

The plan must also define maximum per-run and total retained size, compression,
cleanup/retention policy, expected capture overhead, and a capture-health gate.
A run is incomplete evidence if required artifacts are absent, truncated,
unjoinable, or the manifest says replay is impossible.

## Common bundle

Every failure bundle under `docs/status/evidence/<topic>/<run>/` must include:

- a manifest with exact case/stage, first observed failure, commit/dirty state,
  config, seed or generated-case parameters, clock domain, artifact paths,
  capture window, sizes, missing items, and `capture_complete` /
  `replay_possible` status;
- the exact boundary input or a lossless serialized equivalent, boundary
  output, timestamps and join IDs, state/reason codes, relevant parameters,
  logs, scored summary, and resource/timing trace;
- enough upstream context to recreate the boundary input and enough downstream
  evidence to prove the observed failure;
- a bounded ROS/IPC capture when independent messages must be time-aligned.

Write truth/reference data separately from online inputs so a replay cannot
accidentally feed Gazebo or evaluation truth into production logic. Flush and
verify the manifest before teardown, overwrite, or rerun.

## Perception boundary payloads

Use these boundaries unless the plan defines a more precise pipeline:

1. sensor ingest;
2. stamp synchronization, TF resolution, and motion compensation;
3. RGB/depth preprocessing;
4. segmentation;
5. mask/cloud filtering and joins;
6. support/top/3D geometry detection;
7. published detection and consumer input.

T2 normally preserves the bounded raw RGB, depth, camera info, point cloud and
TF window; synchronized observations; masks and confidence/class metadata;
filtered/cargo clouds; support and geometry candidates; final detection;
per-stage stamps/frame IDs/generation IDs; queue occupancy, drops and reason
codes. Save GT pose/size and errors in a separate reference record. T3 may add
module internals only for the localized suspect, for example mask components,
RANSAC samples/inliers, rejected clusters, or PCA inputs/eigenvalues.

## Planning and execution boundary payloads

Use these boundaries unless the plan defines a more precise pipeline:

1. detected object and placement request;
2. candidate/waypoint generation;
3. TF, robot-state, and PlanningScene snapshot;
4. IK, Cartesian path, collision checking, or MoveGroup request/response;
5. selected and time-parameterized trajectory;
6. controller execution and settling;
7. attach/vacuum/release, placement commit, and orchestrator transition.

T2 normally preserves the complete detected object; original and transformed
targets/waypoints; joint state and IK seed; relevant TF at the request stamp;
normalized container descriptor and `geometry_hash`; oriented payload and swept
geometry; collision objects, attached objects, octomap/cargo map and Allowed
Collision Matrix; every attempted placement candidate and rejection reason;
exact MoveIt request/response, planner/group/end-effector IDs, constraints,
tolerances, timeouts, error codes, Cartesian fraction and fallback decision;
planned trajectory; controller goal/result and timestamped planned-versus-
actual joint trace; final pose/settling error; request IDs, state transitions,
vacuum/attachment state, and commit outcome. T3 may add only localized planner
internals such as all IK candidates, collision contacts/distances, constraint
evaluations, or search diagnostics.

Do not classify `PLACE_CANDIDATE_EXHAUSTED` as `BIN_FULL`: retain every tried
candidate so planning infeasibility remains distinguishable from packing
capacity. Do not classify an occupancy-blocked carry as `BIN_FULL` either:
retain every tried path, occupancy hash/revision, and rejection reason.
